{"error":false,"activeTest":false,"task":{"id":14274469,"type":"Review","status":"Processed","importance":1,"reason":null,"locked":null,"proofId":null,"currentQuestionId":null,"currentQuestionTimestamp":null,"progress":1,"points":4,"passed":1,"rawPointsAwarded":2,"pointsAwarded":2,"mp":null,"mpAwarded":null,"mpDecayRate":null,"student":{"id":57964,"firstName":"William","lastName":"Parker","locale":null,"subscriptionId":"sub_1UO2MGD4gcTFRx970s7DzjQk","sequenceId":15},"course":{"id":76,"name":"Methods of Proof"},"topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"test":{"id":null,"name":null,"diagnostic":null,"timeLimit":null,"questionCount":null},"created":"2026-10-16T23:05:45.000Z","started":"2026-10-16T23:53:27.000Z","completed":"2026-10-16T23:54:52.000Z","processed":"2026-10-16T23:55:06.000Z","progressStr":"100%","answers":[{"id":62411479,"created":"2026-10-16T23:53:40.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12395,"number":1,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 11:53 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:12","question":{"id":37778,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5510,"text":"<p>Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$</p>\n","choice1":"$3$","choice2":"$5$","choice3":"$2$","choice4":"$-2$","choice5":"$-3$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\\begin{align}\n\t\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\n\t\t2-\\lambda & 3 \\\\\n\t\t1 & 2-\\lambda\n\t\\end{array}\\right| &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t(2-\\lambda)(2-\\lambda)-3 &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+1 &= 0\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tA^2-4A+I &= O.\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Since $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tA^{-1}(A^2-4A+I) &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\tA-4I+A^{-1} &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\tA^{-1} &= 4I-A\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Therefore, $p=4,$ $q=-1$ and\n\\[\np+q=4+(-1)=3.\n\\]</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5510,"title":"Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"},"group":{"id":12822,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14274469,"answer":{"id":62411479,"cread":"2026-10-16T23:53:40.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12395},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62411488,"created":"2026-10-16T23:53:57.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":3,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13925,"number":2,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 11:53 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:14","question":{"id":37779,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5510,"text":"<p>Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$</p>\n","choice1":"$\\dfrac{7}{9}$","choice2":"$\\dfrac{4}{9}$","choice3":"$5$","choice4":"$-\\dfrac{3}{7}$","choice5":"$-4$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\t\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\n\t\t1-\\lambda & -2 \\\\\n\t\t1 & 7-\\lambda\n\t\\end{array}\\right| &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t(1-\\lambda)(7-\\lambda)+2 &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\lambda^2-8\\lambda+9 &= 0\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tA^2-8A+9I &= O.\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Since $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tA^{-1}(A^2-8A+9I) &= O \\\\[5pt]\n\tA-8I+9A^{-1} &= O \\\\[5pt]\n\tA^{-1} &= \\dfrac{8}{9}I-\\dfrac{1}{9}A\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Therefore, $p=\\dfrac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{9},$ and </p>\n<p>\\[\np+q=\\dfrac{8}{9}+\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right)=\\dfrac{7}{9}.\n\\]</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5510,"title":"Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"},"group":{"id":12822,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14274469,"answer":{"id":62411488,"cread":"2026-10-16T23:53:57.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":3,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13925},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62411500,"created":"2026-10-16T23:54:15.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13328,"number":3,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 11:54 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":83395,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5511,"text":"<p>Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)=-\\lambda^3+5\\lambda-12.$ Then, $A^3=$</p>\n","choice1":"$5A - 12I$","choice2":"$12A^2 - 5I$","choice3":"$7A^2 - 5I$","choice4":"$-5A + 12I$","choice5":"$-5A - 12I$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tp(A) &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\t-A^3+5A-12I &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\tA^3 &= 5A - 12I.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5511,"title":"Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"},"group":{"id":12823,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14274469,"answer":{"id":62411500,"cread":"2026-10-16T23:54:15.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13328},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62411510,"created":"2026-10-16T23:54:35.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":5,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":16595,"number":4,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 11:54 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:17","question":{"id":140522,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5511,"text":"<p>Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)=-\\lambda^3+5\\lambda-7.$ Then, $A^3=$</p>\n","choice1":"$5A - 7I$","choice2":"$-2A - 7I$","choice3":"$5A - 6I$","choice4":"$3A - 7I$","choice5":"$5A - I$","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tp(A) &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\t-A^3+5A-7I &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\tA^3 &= 5A - 7I.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5511,"title":"Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"},"group":{"id":12823,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14274469,"answer":{"id":62411510,"cread":"2026-10-16T23:54:35.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":5,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":16595},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62411519,"created":"2026-10-16T23:54:51.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12820,"number":5,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 11:54 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":140606,"format":"MC","difficulty":2,"exampleId":5512,"text":"<p>If $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)=x^2+3x-2,$ then $A^4=$</p>\n","choice1":"$-39A+22I$","choice2":"$30A+15I$","choice3":"$-12A+53I$","choice4":"$-21A+2I$","choice5":"$-10A+7I$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>First, let&#39;s divide the polynomial $f(x)=x^4$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)=x^2+3x-2,$ using long division:</p>\n<table rules=all cellpadding=0 align=\"center\" style=\"border:hidden; border-color:black; margin: auto; mar